<exercisegroup cols="2" xml:id="exercisegroup-col-row-null-space-size-from-rank-nullity">
<title> Size versus rank and nullity </title>

<introduction><p>
	In each of the following,
	determine the size of a matrix that has rank <m> r </m>,
	nullity <m> z </m>,
	and whose column space is a subspace of <m> \R^k </m>,
	or state why such a matrix is not possible.
</p></introduction>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>
		<m> r = 4 </m>,
		<m> z = 3 </m>,
		<m> k = 6 </m>
	</p></statement>

	<answer><p><m> 6 \times 7 </m></p></answer>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>
		<m> r = 5 </m>,
		<m> z = 7 </m>,
		<m> k = 8 </m>
	</p></statement>

	<answer><p><m> 8 \times 12 </m></p></answer>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>
		<m> r = 4 </m>,
		<m> z = 0 </m>,
		<m> k = 4 </m>
	</p></statement>

	<answer><p><m> 4 \times 4 </m></p></answer>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>
		<m> r = 6 </m>,
		<m> z = 2 </m>,
		<m> k = 5 </m>
	</p></statement>

	<answer><p>
		Not possible:
		need at least <m> 6 </m> rows to fit rank <m> 6 </m>.
	</p></answer>

</exercise>

</exercisegroup>
